<DocsLayout title="AMD and Intel graphics">
    <h1>AMD and Intel graphics</h1>

    <p class="docs-lead">
        AMD and Intel graphics support is included directly in the Linux
        kernel and Mesa graphics stack used by Octlitch.
    </p>

    <h2>Drivers are already installed</h2>

    <p>
        In normal installations, no separate graphics driver download is
        required for supported AMD or Intel GPUs.
    </p>

    <p>
        Graphics support is provided through the kernel, Mesa and other
        components supplied by Fedora.
    </p>

    <div class="docs-note">
        <strong>Keep Octlitch updated</strong>

        <p>
            Kernel and Mesa updates can provide performance improvements,
            hardware support and graphics fixes.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Update the graphics stack</h2>

    <p>
        Graphics updates arrive through the normal system update process:
    </p>

    <pre><code>sudo dnf upgrade --refresh</code></pre>

    <p>
        Restart after major kernel or graphics-stack updates:
    </p>

    <pre><code>sudo reboot</code></pre>

    <h2>Identify your graphics hardware</h2>

    <p>
        Display the graphics adapters detected by the system:
    </p>

    <pre><code>lspci | grep -Ei 'VGA|3D|Display'</code></pre>

    <p>
        To also show the active kernel driver:
    </p>

    <pre><code>lspci -k | grep -EA3 'VGA|3D|Display'</code></pre>

    <h2>AMD graphics</h2>

    <p>
        Modern AMD GPUs normally use the <code>amdgpu</code> kernel driver.
    </p>

    <p>
        Check whether it is loaded:
    </p>

    <pre><code>lsmod | grep amdgpu</code></pre>

    <p>
        You can also inspect the device and driver association with:
    </p>

    <pre><code>lspci -k | grep -EA3 'VGA|3D|Display'</code></pre>

    <h2>Intel graphics</h2>

    <p>
        Intel integrated graphics use drivers included with the kernel and
        Mesa stack.
    </p>

    <p>
        The exact kernel driver depends on the Intel graphics generation
        and the Fedora kernel in use.
    </p>

    <p>
        Check the active driver with:
    </p>

    <pre><code>lspci -k | grep -EA3 'VGA|3D|Display'</code></pre>

    <h2>Firmware</h2>

    <p>
        Some graphics hardware requires firmware files provided by the
        Fedora firmware packages.
    </p>

    <p>
        Keep the system updated so current firmware is installed:
    </p>

    <pre><code>sudo dnf upgrade --refresh</code></pre>

    <h2>Wayland</h2>

    <p>
        Octlitch uses KDE Plasma with Wayland as its modern display
        environment.
    </p>

    <p>
        Supported AMD and Intel systems normally work with Wayland using
        the drivers already included in the system.
    </p>

    <h2>Troubleshooting graphics problems</h2>

    <p>
        First identify the GPU and active driver:
    </p>

    <pre><code>lspci -k | grep -EA3 'VGA|3D|Display'</code></pre>

    <p>
        Then check kernel messages for graphics-related errors:
    </p>

    <pre><code>journalctl -b -k | grep -Ei 'drm|amdgpu|i915|xe|gpu'</code></pre>

    <p>
        Information about the current session can also be useful:
    </p>

    <pre><code>echo $XDG_SESSION_TYPE</code></pre>

    <p>
        On the standard Octlitch desktop this will normally report:
    </p>

    <pre><code>wayland</code></pre>

